\section{Schema Decomposition}
\label{sec:sdgd}

\looseness=-1
A natural intervention is to partition the schema so confusable types never co-occur (Figure~\ref{fig:sdgd_pipeline}). We tested Schema-DAG-Guided Decomposition (SDGD), which applies greedy graph coloring on the SCS graph to assign types to low-confusability batches. While oracle routing recovers accuracy (0.724 vs.\ 0.504 single-pass), all practical routing strategies fall below the single-pass baseline, and NER decomposition amplifies false positives (F1: 0.445$\to$0.251). Even oracle accuracy falls short of per-type baselines ({>}0.85): merge amplification is the structural bottleneck: union merge doubles false positives (precision: 0.56$\to$0.21) while voting merge sacrifices recall. Confusability-aware batch composition avoids routing entirely and achieves higher accuracy under oracle assignment (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_batch_composition}). Architectures that avoid explicit merge (e.g., retrieval-based type selection) may circumvent this. Full protocol and analysis are in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_sdgd}.

\subsection{Multi-Dimensional Degradation}
\label{sec:multi_channel}

\looseness=-1
Error taxonomy decomposition (Table~\ref{tab:error_taxonomy} and Figure~\ref{fig:error_taxonomy}) shows intra-batch substitution (IntraSub) scales monotonically with BCS across all three datasets ($\rho = 0.843\text{--}1.0$, all $p < 0.001$). In NER, missed rates are higher (43--65\%) and non-monotonic; in DocRED, IntraSub accounts for 72\% of errors in high-BCS batches vs.\ 15\% in low-BCS. Extreme-BCS batches produce $9.9\text{--}27.5\times$ more confusion than low-BCS batches. Cross-model replication confirms the pattern on FewRel RE: Gemini~2.5~Flash ($\rho = 0.821$) and Llama-3.1-8B ($\rho = 0.430$; Table~\ref{tab:cross_model_error_taxonomy}).

